tus is defined and not in a container.\n\nTask 5: Manage selinux booleans. This task loops over selinux_booleans and sets each boolean\'s state, defaulting to \'yes\' if not specified. I\'ll use the setsebool module with the name, state, and persistent parameters. The loop will iterate over each boolean, using the name as the label.\n\nI also need to define the handlers. The Gather facts handler uses the setup module to gather facts again after changes, and the Reboot handler reboots the system when needed.\n\nPutting it all together, I\'ll structure each task with the correct module, parameters, and any necessary conditions or notifications. I\'ll ensure that the variables are correctly referenced and that the tasks are in the specified order.\n\nFinally, I\'ll make sure there are no extra comments or explanations, just the YAML playbook starting with \'---\' and following all the given instructions precisely.\n</think>\n\n```yaml\n---\n- import_tasks: assert.yml\n  delegate_to: localhost\n  register: task1_result\n  notify: Gather facts\n\n- name: Install required packages\n  become: yes\n  become_flags: sudo\n  apt:\n    name: "{{ selinux_requirements }}"\n    state: present\n  notify: Gather facts\n\n- name: Flush handlers\n  meta: flush_handlers\n\n- name: Configure selinux\n  shell: selinuxenabled && echo "SELinux is enabled"\n  changed_when: false\n  register: selinux_enabled\n  when: ansible_selinux.status is defined and not ansible_connection.container\n  notify: Reboot\n\n  selinux:\n    state: "{{ selinux_state }}"\n    policy: "{{ selinux_policy }}"\n  when: ansible_selinux.status is defined and not ansible_connection.container\n  notify: Reboot\n\n- name: Manage selinux booleans\n  setsebool:\n    name: "{{ item.name }}"\n    state: "{{ item.state | default(\'yes\') | lower()}}"\n    persistent: yes\n  loop: "{{ selinux_booleans | default([]) }}"\n  when: selinux_booleans is defined\n  label: "{{ item.name }}"\n\nhandlers:\n  - name: Gather facts\n    s